    <h1>Bran the Blessed and the Ravens of Britain</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> Bran fab Llŷr — whose name means "raven" in Welsh — is a giant-king of Britain in the Second Branch of the <em>Mabinogi</em>. His severed head, miraculously alive, is buried in London to protect the island from invasion. The name, the head, and the London connection have led many to link Bran to the Tower of London raven tradition — though that link is speculative, not documented. What is certain is that "Bran" as "raven" reflects the central importance of the raven in Welsh and Celtic symbolic culture.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>The Second Branch of the Mabinogi</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> Branwen ferch Llŷr ("Branwen daughter of the Sea") — the Second Branch — is one of the four central tales of the <em>Mabinogi</em>, surviving in the White Book of Rhydderch (c. 1350) and the Red Book of Hergest (c. 1382–1410), drawing on oral tradition considerably older. The story's key events:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Bran gives his sister Branwen (her name means "white raven" or "blessed raven") to Matholwch, King of Ireland, in marriage.</li>
      <li>Conflict arises over an insult to Matholwch; Branwen is mistreated in Ireland and trains a starling to carry news to Bran.</li>
      <li>Bran leads an army to Ireland; a catastrophic war destroys almost everyone on both sides.</li>
      <li>Bran is fatally wounded by a poisoned spear (from the Cauldron of Rebirth). He commands his followers to cut off his head.</li>
      <li>Bran's head remains miraculously alive, companionable, and prophetic for 87 years, sustaining its companions.</li>
      <li>The head is finally buried at the White Mount (Gwynfryn) — traditionally identified with Tower Hill, the site of the Tower of London — with its face towards France, to ward off invasion.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Bran as "Raven"</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> The name Bran is well-attested as the common Welsh and Old Irish word for raven (or crow). In Proto-Celtic, the reconstructed form is <em>*branos</em>. Related forms appear in Gaulish personal names and in Irish mythology, where Bran is also a heroic name (the voyage story <em>Immram Brain</em> — "Voyage of Bran" — is a separate Irish narrative).<sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
    <p>Branwen, the sister, carries the diminutive or compound form — <em>Bran</em> + <em>gwen</em> ("white/blessed") — giving "white raven" or "blessed raven." This gendered raven-name for the central female figure reinforces the raven's mythological centrality in the story.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>The Tower of London Connection</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-lore">[lore]</strong> The identification of Bran's burial site with Tower Hill — and the idea that the head's guardianship underlies the Tower raven tradition — is an attractive hypothesis that has circulated since at least the 19th century. However:<sup><a href="#src-3">3</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li>No medieval source explicitly connects Bran's head to the Tower of London ravens.</li>
      <li>Historian Geoffrey Parnell's research found no evidence of the Tower raven tradition before the 1880s.</li>
      <li>The legend "if the ravens leave, the Tower and the kingdom shall fall" is not traceable to any medieval document.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The connection between Bran and the Tower ravens remains a popular and evocative theory — the name, the location, and the protective function all align — but it has not been established from historical evidence. It may be a 19th-century romantic interpretation rather than a surviving ancient tradition.</p>
  </section>

